\documentclass{article}
\title{Bus Headway Reliability Under Conditional Signal Priority}
\author{L. Okafor and T. Lindqvist}
\date{April 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Conditional signal priority grants green extensions only to late buses.
We denote the scheduled headway by $h_s and the realised headway by $h_r$ throughout.
The control target is to keep the headway deviation within one minute
without delaying cross traffic.

\section{Data}
The study uses six months of vehicle location records
from two trunk routes in a mid-sized city.
Each record carries a timestamp, a stop identifier, and the load profile.

\section{Method}
For stop $t$ and run $k$ we form the deviation $d_{t,k}$ and
the running cost $c_t_k$ used by the priority logic.
A run loses priority eligibility once its deviation is negative.

\section{Findings}
The pilot months show three regularities:
\begin{itemize}
\item priority cuts the variance of headway deviations by a fifth;
\item late buses recover half a minute per kilometre on average;
\item cross-street delay stays within the agreed budget.

\section{Discussion}
The recovery effect is strongest where stops are widely spaced,
because dwell time variability dominates on closely spaced stops.

\section{Conclusion}
Conditional priority improves regularity at a modest cost to cross traffic.
\end{document}

